{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
For the last time
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[F]Let's go [Dm]back to  [G7][C7]Al-a-bamy
[F]Let's go [Dm]see my [G7]dear old [C7]Mammy
[F]Fryin' [F#0]eggs and [Bb]cookin’  [B07]hammy
[C7]Say That's what I like about [F]the South
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Well there won't make no mistakey
And your nerves are never shakey
You should taste that layer cake
That's what I like about the South
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Solo}
She's got baked ribs and candied yams
Sugar-cured Virginia hams
A basement full of them berry jams
An'  that's what I like about the South
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Corn bread an' turnip greens
Ham hocks an' buttered beans
Mardi Gras down in New Orleans
And That's what I like about the South
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Solo}
Here comes that man with all the news
Bocks backed coat and buttoned shoes
Paid up with his union dues
And That's what I like about the South
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
Well there's a place down south called Doo-wah-diddy
It ain't no town  and it ain't no city
It’s pretty, it sure is pretty
Doo - wah - diddy
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Solo}
Well every time I pass your door
You act like you don’t want me now more
Just shake and head inside
And I’ll go truckin right on by
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
I ain’t here to criticize
And I’m not here to sympathize
Don’t tell me them no good lies
Because lyin gals I do despise
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 7}
Solo until last line in form then sing:
[C7]That's what I like about the [F]South
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 8}
Alternate lyrics
Now way down west the cane grows tall
Down where they   say "you all"
Walk right in with that Southern drawl
That's what I like about the South
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 9}
Here come Lance he's got the news
And baby needs a pair of shoes
He's caught up with his union dues
An'  that's what I like about the South
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 10}
There goes ol' Billy Pete
You can hear those chunkin' feet
He would rather tote than eat
An'  that's what I  like about the South
{end_of_verse}
